---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, GITHUB_URL, SITE_TITLE, X_HANDLE, X_URL } from "../consts";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Who runs nano-api, how it is built, and what that means for you as a customer."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="page">
				<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
				<h1>A collection of small precise tools.</h1>

				<p>
					nano-api is a family of single-purpose APIs. Each one lives on its own subdomain, takes one
					API key, and does one job you would otherwise have to run a server for. The first is
					<strong>NanoPulse</strong>: it tells you when a job that is supposed to run has quietly
					stopped running.
				</p>

				<h2>How it is built</h2>
				<p>
					Cloudflare Workers at the edge, D1 for storage, and a scheduled trigger that does the
					counting. No servers, no containers, no queue to babysit. That is why the free plan can
					exist: the marginal cost of one more monitor is close to nothing, so there is no reason to
					meter you into a corner.
				</p>
				<p>
					API keys are stored as SHA-256 hashes and never in clear text — if the database leaked
					tomorrow, nobody could ping as you. Keys are yours to rotate and revoke.
				</p>

				<h2>What you can expect</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>Answers from the person who wrote the code, usually the same day.</li>
					<li>Honest limits. If something is not built, the page says so instead of hinting.</li>
					<li>
						No dark patterns: no annual-only discounts hiding the monthly price, no "contact sales"
						wall in front of the numbers.
					</li>
					<li>
						No deletion of your data as a retention tactic. Delete a monitor and it is gone,
						including its history.
					</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>What you should not expect</h2>
				<p>
					An enterprise SLA, phone support, or a status page with nine nines. This is a small,
					carefully built service run by one person alongside a day job. For monitoring where a
					missed alert has serious consequences, run two providers — I would tell a friend the same
					thing.
				</p>

				<h2>Get in touch</h2>
				<p>
					<a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> for keys, questions, invoices and
					bug reports, or <a href={X_URL} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{X_HANDLE}</a> on X if that
					is easier. Either way it reaches the person who wrote the code.
				</p>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page {
		max-width: 44em;
		padding: 2em 0 0;
	}
	.page h2 {
		margin-top: 2em;
		font-size: 1.4rem;
	}
	.page p,
	.page li {
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	.page ul {
		padding-left: 1.2em;
	}
	.page li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5em;
	}
</style>
